.TH XUR.STD.LIST 7 "2026-10-29" "xur 0.1.2" "Xur Manual"
.SH NAME
xur.std.list \- list methods
.SH DESCRIPTION
Lists in Xur are shared and change in place, like Python's.
.B std::list
is not imported directly; instead these are methods on any list value.
.SH METHODS
.TP
.B len() -> i64
Number of elements.
.TP
.B is_empty() -> bool
Whether the list has no elements.
.TP
.B push(item)
Append an item to the end.
.TP
.B pop() -> Option<Value>
Remove and return the last item.
.TP
.B get(index) -> Option<Value>
Return the item at
.IR index ,
or
.B None
if out of bounds.
.TP
.B first() -> Option<Value>
The first item.
.TP
.B last() -> Option<Value>
The last item.
.TP
.B insert(index, item)
Insert an item at the given position.
.TP
.B remove(index) -> Value
Remove and return the item at the given position.
.TP
.B contains(item) -> bool
Whether the list contains the item.
.TP
.B clear()
Remove all items.
.TP
.B reverse()
Reverse the list in place.
.TP
.B extend(other)
Append all items from another list. Safe to call as
.BR xs.extend(xs) .
.TP
.B to_hex() -> str
Format a list of bytes as a hex string.
.TP
.B join(separator) -> str
Join all items into a string, separated by
.IR separator .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
use std::io;

fn main() {
    let xs = [1, 2, 3];
    xs.push(4);
    io::println("len: {}", xs.len());
    io::println("last: {}", xs.last().unwrap());
    io::println("joined: {}", xs.join(", "));
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xur.std (7),
.BR xur.std.bytes (7)
